          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p3\",\"quote\":\"many cases their positions in this list are approximate, as there is frequently a large uncertainty in their estimated diameters due to their distance from Earth. There are uncertainties in the figures for mass and radius, and irregularities in the shape and density, with accuracy often depending on how close the object is to Earth or whether it has been visited by a probe.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p6\",\"quote\":\"There can be difficulty in determining the diameter (within a factor of about 2) for typical objects beyond Saturn\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p16\",\"quote\":\"Many intrinsically bright TNOs like 2018 VG 18 and 2017 OF 201 do not have directly measured sizes (e.g. via stellar occultation and radiometry of thermal emission ), so their sizes are estimated based on an assumed albedo .\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p31\",\"quote\":\"Radius has been determined by various methods, such as optical ( Hubble ), thermal ( Spitzer ), or direct imaging via spacecraft\"}]}"
